      "file": "scenes/scene-1.js",
      "narration": "Every video file has a container, like dot mp4, wrapping compressed video and audio. You don't need codec names. Just ask whether the destination can play it.",
      "storyboard": "Paper stage, close-up. The file icon becomes a cutaway box: on 'container' the outer shell draws on labeled '.mp4'; on 'compressed video and audio' two inner layers slide in, a film strip and a waveform. On 'codec names', tiny grey jargon labels flutter in then get swept away. On 'can the destination play it?' the box rotates toward a play button that lights amber; headline 'Can it play there?' holds. The bullet list is cut.",
      "minDuration": 10,
      "slide": {
        "title": "A video file has two layers",
        "text": "Container: the file type, like .mp4 or .webm. Codecs: how video and audio are compressed. What matters: can the destination play it?",
        "notes": "Every video file has a container, like dot mp4, wrapping compressed video and audio. You don't need codec names. Just ask whether the destination can play it.",
